# Vulnerability details

## Impact
For each RDPX token used to bond, the protocol will buy a put option at a strike price of currentPrice * 75%, roundup to nearest precision value. However, the implementation of roundUp() in some cases can return a strike price even larger than current price, which makes no sense and can make the user loss money.

## Proof of Concept
Let's look at the roundUp() function:

    function roundUp(uint256 _strike) public view returns (uint256 strike) {
        uint256 remainder = _strike % roundingPrecision;
        if (remainder == 0) {
          return _strike;
        } else {
          return _strike - remainder + roundingPrecision;
        }
    }

The roundingPrecision is set to 1e6

    uint256 public roundingPrecision = 1e6;

We can find that as long as _strike price is less than roundingPrecision (1e6), the remainder will be equal to _strike, and the returned result will always be 1e6. 

In purchase() function, the input value to roundUp() is currentPrice - (currentPrice / 4)

    uint256 strike = roundUp(currentPrice - (currentPrice / 4)); // 25% below the current price

So as long as currentPrice is less than 1e6, currentPrice - (currentPrice / 4) will also be less than 1e6, so the returned strike price is 1e6, which is larger than the currentPrice! 

And it's pretty reasonable to assume currentPrice is under 1e6. Since the oracle will return a price in 1e8 decimals, if 1 Wei of RDPX = 1 Wei of Eth, the price is 1e8, if 1 RDPX < 0.01 Eth, the price will be less than 1e6, we can see that 1 RDPX = 0.01 Eth is a reasonable price.

Also, currently there is no way to update roundingPrecision to other values.

